The iodine generated by the anode reacts with water to form hydroiodic acid until the completion of the reaction of all the water, and the end of the reaction is indicated by a detection unit composed of a pair of platinum electrodes. According to Faraday's law of electrolysis, the number of molecules of iodine participating in the reaction is equal to the number of molecules of water, which is proportional to the amount of electric charge.
