Maximizing packaging space took on new importance as carries began to charge shippers based on dimensional weight in 2014. Early research on the switch showed it could impact between 70% to 80% of shipments and result in about a 30% to 35% increase in shipping costs, according to Manjeet Singh, the director of global operations science and analytics at DHL.
Ehsan Ardjmand, the study’s co-author and assistant professor of analytics and information systems at Ohio University, said the research showed how carton set optimization can help warehouses cut down on their carbon footprint by reducing packaging and the number of shipments needed.
Singh and Ardjmand used historical order information from e-commerce warehouses to help determine this optimal set of cartons.
“After that, we use our tool and figure out, what are the right size cartons or boxes that this warehouse or this customer should keep so that it minimizes the shipment spend?” Singh said.
Order cubing: Determine the best fit carton for each order in a sample.
Selecting the optimal carton set: This process involves a type of algorithm, known as an evolutionary algorithm, that will go through different carton combinations to see which set will minimize shipping costs.
This means it might recommend that a shipper use a set of 15 different carton sizes, but it will also provide the best size mix for 14 or 13 cartons that would still be able to handle their orders. The researchers found that as the number of carton sizes increases, so does carton utilization and annual savings — but that doesn’t mean it’s always the right call.
Sometimes the result of this analysis is that a warehouse has such high variability among its order sizes that it would actually benefit from investing in its own carton-cutting machine that would make containers specifically for any given order, the researchers said.
