There is no need to specially mention that after knee surgery, the patient will encounter challenges and severe pain on the path of their recovery. Though it will seem very difficult, and maybe even impossible at times, it is vital to try to remember that what happens after the knee replacement surgery is just as imperative to the overall success of the procedure as the surgery itself.
While the patient may experience some sort of pain and frustration, it is important to keep in mind that some health experts say 50 percent of a positive outcome is completely dependent on the patient’s compliance to work hard in physical therapy and rehab .
The result will be so worth the effort. Taking medications as prescribed and getting plenty of sleep is also very important to help the knee heal properly after the surgery.
According to several health researchers, it is proved that the swelling after total knee replacement surgery can last for three to six months.
After the initial swelling has calmed down a bit, the patient can alternate between ice and heat in order to relax the muscles and ease the stiffness. The patient is advised to speak to the doctor or physical therapist if ice is not helping with swelling, or if the patient feels like the heat or ice is not benefiting them, for a longer period of time.
Keeping the body healthy will encourage speeding up the healing process after surgery. If the patient has the habit of smoking, they have to make an effort to cut back or stop; smoking shrinks the blood vessels, which makes the healing process much slower.
And finally, strengthening the muscles in the legs and improving overall blood flow by participating in low-impact activities such as walking, swimming, or peddling a stationary bike once the doctor gives the patient the required permission. However, it is wise to limit or refrain from exercises that can cause knee pain until fully recovered.
In order to help the patient be mindful of their form when they bend down, kneel and sit, it’s a great idea to review these movements with the physical therapist so that he or she can assist the patient to learn how to do them safely.
Not only in total knee replacement surgery but also in any kind of surgery, the patients are not advised to take up any other surgeries while they are still recovering from the previous procedures.
Don’t give up on yourself and the healing process.
Be gentle with yourself and give yourself appreciation for the accomplishments that you have made along the way of the healing process. If you experience any uncomfortable and serious events such as chest pain, loss of consciousness or altered mental state, shortness of breath, loss of color or warmth to your limb, fall, or are unable to move your operative limb, please go to the nearest Emergency Department immediately.
If you experience any uncomfortable and serious events such as chest pain, loss of consciousness or altered mental state, shortness of breath, loss of color or warmth to your limb, fall, or are unable to move your operative limb, please go to the nearest Emergency Department immediately.
